e_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042597/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9f2bd1e5820ef30e2966e0b6a368692000043a825cb5bcdc1f1a6f03f2ff15"} {"question" "q-159599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8bfa07d7e8e9bd2f54ce8367d28161cedf0532fadd238e5e37089ea1c8d04126"} {"question" "q-159599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042597/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9f2bd1e5820ef30e2966e0b6a368692000043a825cb5bcdc1f1a6f03f2ff15"} {"question" "q-159599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2e"] ["math" "2e+1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}e+1"] ["math" "e+1"] ["math" "{e}^{2}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042597/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9f2bd1e5820ef30e2966e0b6a368692000043a825cb5bcdc1f1a6f03f2ff15"} {"question" "q-159599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2e+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042597/state.json" "file_sha256" "da9f2bd1e5820ef30e2966e0b6a368692000043a825cb5bcdc1f1a6f03f2ff15"} {"question" "e-11045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y) = y^2\\ln{x},$ express $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ as the limit of a difference quotient." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042597/example-11045.json" "file_sha256" "76becbc7c99dd7f1205da960f2f53e50890b33c2f9a60d9f3bed76d9701f7be2"} {"question" "e-11045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\left(\\dfrac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x) = y^2 \\ln{x},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y) = y^2\\ln(x+h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0}\\left( \\dfrac{y^2\\ln(x+h) -y^2\\ln{x} }{h}\\right).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042597/example-11045.json" "file_sha256" "76becbc7c99dd7f1205da960f2f53e50890b33c2f9a60d9f3bed76d9701f7be2"} {"question" "e-11026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ given that $f(x,y) = 3x\\ln y + 2ye^x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042597/example-11026.json" "file_sha256" "4f5c8ce0cadc22a413b0458a351aca559b1f72869925f987ce1f8c0c3dc6fd57"} {"question" "e-11026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =3x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+2{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & =3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}+2{e}^{x}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{y}+2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042597/example-11026.json" "file_sha256" "4f5c8ce0cadc22a413b0458a351aca559b1f72869925f987ce1f8c0c3dc6fd57"} {"question" "e-11049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x,y) = x^2y^2+\\sin{x}\\cos{y},$ evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042597/example-11049.json" "file_sha256" "f4b1ab11b35c68d4df843d20925a691b8f284f76f558ae8eddbb1e28a25d81ca"} {"question" "e-11049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,2\\pi )} & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot (2\\pi )^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4{\\pi }^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8{\\pi }^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042597/example-11049.json" "file_sha256" "f4b1ab11b35c68d4df843d20925a691b8f284f76f558ae8eddbb1e28a25d81ca"}] "usage" {"e-11026" [] "e-11049" [] "q-35709" [] "e-11045" []} "basis" 1561 "retractions" [[17592186124817 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ given that $f(x, y) = x^{2}\\sin y + xy$."] [17592186124119 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y) = y^2\\ln{x}$, express $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ as the limit of a difference quotient."] [17592186124119 :question/worked-solution "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\left(\\dfrac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(x) = y^2 \\ln{x}\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\nf(x+h,y) = y^2\\ln(x+h)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0}\\left(\\dfrac{y^2\\ln(x+h) -y^2\\ln{x}}{h}\\right)\n$$"] [17592186124115 :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ given that $f(x,y) = 3x\\ln y + 2ye^x$."] [17592186124115 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y$, treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x\\ln y + 2ye^{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x\\ln y) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2ye^{x}) \\\\\n&= 3x \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln y) + 2e^{x} \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\\n&= 3x \\cdot \\frac{1}{y} + 2e^{x} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3x}{y} + 2e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124120 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y) = x^2y^2+\\sin{x}\\cos{y}$, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi)$."] [17592186124120 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x$, treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y^{2} + \\sin x\\cos y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y^{2}) + \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sin x\\cos y) \\\\\n&= y^{2} \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}) + \\cos y \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sin x) \\\\\n&= y^{2} \\cdot (2x) + \\cos y \\cdot (\\cos x) \\\\\n&= 2xy^{2} + \\cos x\\cos y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\mid _{(π, 2π)} &= 2 \\cdot π \\cdot (2π)^{2} + \\cos π \\cdot \\cos 2π \\\\\n&= 2π \\cdot 4π^{2} + (-1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 8π^{3} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186121925 17592186121926 17592186121927 17592186121928 17592186121929] "immutable_fields" [[17592186121924 :answer-field/correct] [17592186121924 :answer-field/id] [17592186121924 :answer-field/key] [17592186121924 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
